Arduino provides two important functions for working with analog signals:

analogRead() – Used to read analog input signals from sensors.
analogWrite() – Used to generate a PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) signal that simulates an analog output.

Although their names are similar, they perform completely different tasks.
1. analogRead()
Definition

analogRead() is used to read the voltage applied to one of the Arduino's analog input pins (A0–A5 on the Arduino Uno). The Arduino converts the analog voltage into a digital value using its built-in 10-bit Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC).

Syntax
int sensorValue = analogRead(A0);

PRACTICAL EXAMPLE: sMART IRRIGATION SYSTEM

2. analogWrite()
Definition

analogWrite() is used to generate a PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) signal on specific PWM-capable pins. It does not produce a true analog voltage. Instead, it rapidly switches the pin between HIGH and LOW to create an average output voltage.

Syntax
analogWrite(9, 128);

PRACTICAL EXAMPLE:AUTOMATIC STREET LIGHTNING




